# Client credential for the nightly Redis backup - managed by Ansible.
# Copyright 2025-2026 mrveiss
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE, AND WHY IT IS NOT RENDERED FROM code_only.yml (#17932).
#
# `redis-backup.sh` is deployed world-readable (0755) and is rendered on the
# CODE-ONLY update path, where `redis_password` resolves from
# `vault_redis_password` -- which no inventory in this repository defines. The
# "DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED" ruling at the top of roles/redis/tasks/code_only.yml
# therefore keeps every artifact whose CONTENT depends on that variable off the
# update path, because a code-only re-render would emit it without the credential
# and silently strip authentication from the live data store.
#
# Substituting the credential into the backup script would reverse that ruling
# twice over: it would put a secret in a 0755 file, and it would put it in a file
# the updater re-renders. So the script carries only this file's PATH -- a
# constant -- and reads the value here at run time. This file is 0600 root and is
# written on the PROVISIONING path only (roles/redis/tasks/main.yml), where the
# credential is resolvable; the updater never touches it, so an update cannot
# empty it.
#
# `REDISCLI_AUTH` rather than `-a`: redis-cli reads it from the environment, so
# the value never appears on the process list where any local user can read it.
# An empty value here means the server is nopass -- the script checks before
# exporting, because redis-cli sends AUTH with an empty string if the variable is
# set but blank, which a nopass server rejects.
#
# PARSED, NOT SOURCED. The script reads these two lines with `sed`; it never
# `.`-sources this file. Sourcing would execute whatever a credential happens to
# contain and would need shell quoting here, which cannot be written with plain
# Jinja without an Ansible filter -- and this template is rendered by the guard
# with plain jinja2 on purpose. A plain `KEY=value` line needs no quoting at all,
# which is also the shape `_shared/tasks/read_redis_password.yml` reads from
# slm-secrets.env.
REDISCLI_AUTH={{ redis_client_password | default('', true) }}
REDIS_USERNAME={{ redis_username | default('', true) }}
